VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-035
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe health anxiety and panic attack triggered by discovering a benign anatomical variant, requiring immediate physical examination and imaging referral for reassurance.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an asymptomatic patient who incidentally palpated their own xiphoid process while lying in bed tonight and experienced a severe, catastrophic panic attack, convinced they had discovered a massive, lethal gastric tumor. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to physically palpate the abdomen, definitively diagnose the normal bony anatomy, provide immediate psychiatric de-escalation, and issue a routine imaging request to entirely neutralize the pathological health anxiety. Delaying assessment would have resulted in an unwarranted emergency department presentation by a severely panicked patient.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I was lying in bed, touched my stomach, and found a huge, hard, pointed tumor right in the middle. I am terrified, I can't breathe."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents as largely asymptomatic but is requesting immediate screening and medical imaging due to an acute panic attack. The patient has an absolute absence of new acute symptoms such as weight loss, hematemesis, or abdominal pain prior to palpating the structure. A review of previous baseline investigations reveals no history of malignancy. The patient requires a formal radiology request form (ultrasound) not for acute medical necessity, but as a critical psychological intervention to prove the benign nature of the finding and arrest the severe anxiety. The consultation involves safety netting regarding result follow-up.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but currently pacing and hyperventilating.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute health anxiety causing extreme distress.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Highly anxious, hyperventilating slightly, immediately pointing to the epigastrium upon the clinician's arrival.
Physical Exam: Abdominal: Soft, non-tender. Palpation of the epigastrium directly over the area of concern reveals a hard, fixed, pointed midline structure perfectly consistent with a normal xiphoid process. There are no palpable masses, hepatomegaly, or splenomegaly. The presentation involves a normal baseline physical examination and a total lack of immediate therapeutic intervention regarding the physical finding. Expected investigation results include normal or stable physiological parameters on the requested routine pathology or imaging, confirming stable health status and the absence of any pathological mass.
Environmental Observations: Safe, quiet environment; patient was preparing for bed when the panic began.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Request for Routine Medical Imaging (Secondary to Acute Health Anxiety / Normal Variant) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Gastric Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The palpated structure is definitively bony, midline, and anatomically consistent with the xiphoid process. Complete absence of clinical symptoms (weight loss, vomiting, anemia).

Epigastric Hernia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The structure is hard, non-reducible, completely non-tender, and fixed to the sternum, unlike a soft tissue fascial defect.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia explicitly linked to the psychological terror of finding a "tumor", resolving immediately upon clinical reassurance.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a severe, acute panic attack driven by the incidental palpation of completely normal human anatomy (the xiphoid process), catastrophizing it as a terminal malignancy. Telephone triage correctly identified that the patient's severe autonomic hyperarousal required medical evaluation. Urgent face-to-face assessment was mandatory to physically perform an abdominal examination, definitively identify the structure as normal bone, and immediately de-escalate the psychiatric crisis. Providing a routine ultrasound referral acted as a definitive psychological safety net, entirely resolving the panic and preventing the patient from presenting to an emergency department in a state of terror.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted a targeted physical examination of the abdomen and chest wall. Provided definitive verbal reassurance regarding normal human anatomy. Issued a routine, non-urgent referral for an abdominal wall ultrasound strictly for psychological reassurance.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage future acute health anxiety, commit to enforcing a strict 24-hour "wait and see" rule for any newly discovered, painless bodily lump or bump before searching the internet or panicking. Write down the finding on a piece of paper, place it in a drawer, and physically do not look at the area again until the next evening. By enforcing a mandatory delay, you actively prevent the immediate spiral of catastrophic thinking and allow rational observation to take over.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review PRN following the completion of the non-urgent ultrasound.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Seek immediate emergency medical care if you develop sudden, severe, doubling-over abdominal pain, if you begin vomiting blood, or if your stools become black and tarry.

